WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.245
Welcome.

2
00:00:02.995 --> 00:00:09.886
Before you touch a single cable, you need to
know about one kind of document: the DRS.

3
00:00:11.236 --> 00:00:18.308
We will cover what a DRS is, what is in it,
the standards it names, how it fits with the

4
00:00:18.308 --> 00:00:25.541
guide and the cutsheet, how to get the right
one, how to read one, and the rule for having

5
00:00:25.541 --> 00:00:26.425
it on site.

6
00:00:30.575 --> 00:00:34.522
DRS stands for Deployment Requirement Sheet.

7
00:00:36.122 --> 00:00:43.461
A DRS is one of Fluidstack's technical
standards for how installation work must be

8
00:00:43.461 --> 00:00:47.578
done, in every data center Fluidstack deploys.

9
00:00:48.928 --> 00:00:51.488
Here is the most important point.

10
00:00:52.238 --> 00:00:55.651
DRS documents are not guidelines.

11
00:00:56.401 --> 00:00:58.044
They are requirements.

12
00:00:58.794 --> 00:01:00.885
A guideline is advice.

13
00:01:01.635 --> 00:01:05.859
A requirement is the way it must be done,
every time.

14
00:01:07.459 --> 00:01:14.797
Every task you do on site has a matching DRS
that says exactly how it must be done.

15
00:01:15.547 --> 00:01:20.945
If something on site does not seem to fit the
DRS, stop and ask.

16
00:01:25.095 --> 00:01:32.952
A DRS contains step-by-step installation
requirements, technical specifications, the

17
00:01:32.952 --> 00:01:36.039
approved materials, and diagrams.

18
00:01:37.389 --> 00:01:41.997
It also points to the industry standards the
work must meet.

19
00:01:42.747 --> 00:01:44.709
We will look at those next.

20
00:01:46.059 --> 00:01:47.787
Why does it exist?

21
00:01:48.537 --> 00:01:55.801
So every deployment meets the same quality
standard, no matter which contractor or which

22
00:01:55.801 --> 00:01:57.369
site does the work.

23
00:01:58.119 --> 00:02:05.211
That protects the equipment and lowers the
risk of an outage, which means a network or

24
00:02:05.211 --> 00:02:06.695
system going down.

25
00:02:08.045 --> 00:02:14.381
Every DRS is reviewed and approved by
Fluidstack ICT leadership.

26
00:02:15.131 --> 00:02:20.294
ICT means information and communications
technology.

27
00:02:21.644 --> 00:02:23.884
Standards change over time.

28
00:02:24.634 --> 00:02:31.979
When one changes, a new revision of the DRS is
issued, and you are notified through the

29
00:02:31.979 --> 00:02:32.570
portal.

30
00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.645
Always use the current version.

31
00:02:36.395 --> 00:02:39.553
Check the revision date on the cover page.

32
00:02:43.703 --> 00:02:49.228
These are the industry standards named in the
DRS sheets so far.

33
00:02:49.978 --> 00:02:52.495
You do not need to memorize them.

34
00:02:53.245 --> 00:02:56.765
The DRS turns them into the rules you follow.

35
00:02:57.515 --> 00:02:59.862
But you should recognize the names.

36
00:03:01.462 --> 00:03:09.807
ANSI/TIA standards are written by the
Telecommunications Industry Association, and

37
00:03:09.807 --> 00:03:13.878
approved as American National Standards.

38
00:03:16.178 --> 00:03:26.034
TIA-568 covers telecom cabling in buildings,
such as how cable is installed and supported.

39
00:03:27.384 --> 00:03:35.601
TIA-606 covers administration: the
identifiers, labels and records that let

40
00:03:35.601 --> 00:03:37.901
anyone trace a cable.

41
00:03:39.251 --> 00:03:47.251
And TIA-942 covers the infrastructure of data
centers and computer rooms.

42
00:03:48.601 --> 00:03:55.641
The NEC, the National Electrical Code, is the
U.S. electrical safety code.

43
00:03:56.391 --> 00:04:02.834
It sets the fire ratings printed on cable
jackets, such as plenum and riser.

44
00:04:05.134 --> 00:04:12.614
BICSI is a professional association for this
industry that writes design and installation

45
00:04:12.614 --> 00:04:13.454
standards.

46
00:04:14.204 --> 00:04:21.936
NEMA, the National Electrical Manufacturers
Association, sets standards for electrical

47
00:04:21.936 --> 00:04:25.532
products like enclosures and cable tray.

48
00:04:26.882 --> 00:04:30.551
On site, you cite the DRS rule itself.

49
00:04:31.301 --> 00:04:37.659
For example: DRS 01.00.01, rule 2.

50
00:04:41.809 --> 00:04:46.801
So how does the DRS fit with the other
documents you will use?

51
00:04:48.001 --> 00:04:51.307
The DRS says how each task must be done.

52
00:04:52.657 --> 00:04:55.644
The cutsheet says where each cable goes.

53
00:04:56.394 --> 00:04:59.146
It is the final word on any port.

54
00:05:00.496 --> 00:05:07.591
The installation guide was built from the DRS,
and from the patch panel data behind the

55
00:05:07.591 --> 00:05:08.325
cutsheet.

56
00:05:09.675 --> 00:05:13.494
This course, and the Field Kit app, are
training aids.

57
00:05:14.244 --> 00:05:16.313
They explain the documents.

58
00:05:17.063 --> 00:05:20.263
They never replace the DRS on site.

59
00:05:21.613 --> 00:05:29.613
If this course or the guide ever differs from
the current DRS, the DRS is the requirement.

60
00:05:30.363 --> 00:05:33.585
Follow it, and tell your lead about the
difference.

61
00:05:37.735 --> 00:05:41.767
Before each task, find the DRS for that task.

62
00:05:43.117 --> 00:05:47.383
The primary source is Fluidstack's Confluence
portal.

63
00:05:48.133 --> 00:05:56.503
Log in at fluidstack.atlassian.net, open the
LVC Deployment space, then Deployment

64
00:05:56.503 --> 00:05:58.544
Requirements Sheets.

65
00:06:00.844 --> 00:06:08.844
If you cannot reach the portal, contact your
assigned Fluidstack project manager, your PM,

66
00:06:08.844 --> 00:06:10.977
or your site supervisor.

67
00:06:11.727 --> 00:06:15.546
They can send you the document or restore your
access.

68
00:06:16.896 --> 00:06:18.624
Then check the version.

69
00:06:19.374 --> 00:06:25.411
Look at the revision date on the cover page,
and make sure it is the current revision.

70
00:06:26.761 --> 00:06:32.265
Have it on hand where you work: printed, or on
a device you can read there.

71
00:06:33.015 --> 00:06:34.637
Then you can start.

72
00:06:35.987 --> 00:06:37.096
One tip.

73
00:06:37.846 --> 00:06:44.246
The portal needs a login and a connection, and
data halls often have weak signal.

74
00:06:44.996 --> 00:06:50.628
Get or print the DRS for the day's tasks
before you go onto the floor.

75
00:06:54.778 --> 00:06:57.957
Here is how a DRS sheet is laid out.

76
00:06:58.707 --> 00:07:09.160
This is a redrawn version of DRS 01.00.01, the
general cable dressing sheet.

77
00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:12.501
Every sheet has a number.

78
00:07:13.251 --> 00:07:17.518
This one is 01.00.01.

79
00:07:18.268 --> 00:07:22.513
Use the number to find it, to ask about it,
and to cite it.

80
00:07:23.863 --> 00:07:29.495
The title says which task it covers: General
Fiber Cable Dressing.

81
00:07:30.845 --> 00:07:33.149
Next is the revision date.

82
00:07:33.899 --> 00:07:38.273
This sheet was revised on 6/9/2026.

83
00:07:39.023 --> 00:07:41.881
Check it against the portal every time.

84
00:07:43.231 --> 00:07:47.583
The approval block shows who approved the
sheet for Fluidstack.

85
00:07:48.933 --> 00:07:52.624
And every sheet is marked Internal Use Only.

86
00:07:53.374 --> 00:07:55.187
It is confidential.

87
00:07:55.937 --> 00:07:59.564
Use it for the work, and do not share or post
it.

88
00:08:00.914 --> 00:08:03.282
The body holds numbered rules.

89
00:08:04.032 --> 00:08:06.571
A crossed-out picture means never.

90
00:08:07.321 --> 00:08:10.307
Here, never use nylon zip ties.

91
00:08:11.657 --> 00:08:14.068
A sheet can point to another sheet.

92
00:08:14.818 --> 00:08:23.351
This one says to see DRS 01.00.07 for fiber
bend radius.

93
00:08:24.101 --> 00:08:27.579
When you do that part of the work, get that
sheet too.

94
00:08:31.729 --> 00:08:33.563
Now the on-site rule.

95
00:08:34.563 --> 00:08:39.769
You must have the DRS for the work on hand at
the work location.

96
00:08:40.519 --> 00:08:46.023
A printed copy, or a digital copy on a device
you can read there.

97
00:08:47.373 --> 00:08:54.387
Working from memory alone is not acceptable,
even if you have done the task a hundred

98
00:08:54.387 --> 00:08:54.882
times.

99
00:08:56.232 --> 00:09:01.757
If you do not have the DRS, stop work, and get
it before you go on.

100
00:09:03.107 --> 00:09:07.481
This is a Fluidstack site requirement on all
deployments.

101
00:09:11.631 --> 00:09:13.551
Three common mistakes.

102
00:09:14.351 --> 00:09:16.505
Working from an old printout.

103
00:09:17.255 --> 00:09:22.375
A newer revision may have changed a rule, so
check the revision date.

104
00:09:23.725 --> 00:09:31.085
Using this course, a photo from another site,
or a coworker's word instead of the DRS.

105
00:09:31.835 --> 00:09:34.545
Only the current DRS counts.

106
00:09:35.895 --> 00:09:39.095
And sharing a DRS outside the job.

107
00:09:39.845 --> 00:09:42.127
It is Internal Use Only.

108
00:09:46.277 --> 00:09:53.834
In lesson zero of the course, put the
before-you-start steps in order, explore a DRS

109
00:09:53.834 --> 00:09:59.141
sheet, and decide what to do in a few
realistic situations.

110
00:09:59.891 --> 00:10:01.982
Then take the lesson check.

